<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  Long-running cancellable operation, à la PackageKit transactions. The
  agent exports one Operation1 object per in-flight call (ReadIdentity,
  GetPhoto, future Sign / PIN management). Clients connect to Finished
  before reading any properties to avoid lost-update races.

  Stable wire contract:
    Phase enum (uint32):
       0 Created          object queued behind another op on this reader
       1 Connecting       opening LM session
       2 AwaitingConsent  prompter dialog up
       3 Authenticating   LM activating authenticated channel
       4 Reading          LM reading card data
       5 Signing          LM producing the signature on the card
       6 Timestamping     LM applying the RFC-3161 timestamp (b-t)
       7 Done             terminal; matches the moment Finished fired

    Finished.status enum (uint32):
       0 Ok           operation completed normally
       1 Cancelled    operation cancelled (by client, by NameOwnerChanged, or by user-cancel in prompter)
       2 Error        operation failed; errorCode names the category

    Finished.errorCode: stable, append-only taxonomy; never renumber.
    Always 0 when status == Ok or Cancelled; non-zero only when status ==
    Error. The enumeration below is the canonical wire contract for the
    taxonomy: a new code lands here first, the agent-side ErrorCode enum
    follows, and client mirrors update on this file's diffs. The agent
    build pins its enum against this list value-for-value
    (agent/tests/Operation1ErrorCodeXmlGuardTest.cpp), so enum and
    contract cannot drift apart silently:
       0  None
       1  CardRemoved
       2  CredentialWrong
       3  CredentialBlocked
       4  CommunicationError
       5  ParseError
       6  UnsupportedCard
       7  AuthFailed
       8  PrompterError
       9  CapabilityMissing
      10  WatchdogTimeout
      11  KeyNotFound          chosen cert not resolvable / certId mismatch (Sign)
      12  KeyAmbiguous         more than one key matched the discriminator (Sign)
      13  CertExpiredBlocked   expired signer blocked by the per-level policy (Sign)
      14  ChainIncomplete      chain could not be completed/validated (Sign, LT family)
      15  TsaUnreachable       timestamp authority unreachable/unconfigured (Sign)
      16  SigningEngineError   signing engine failure not otherwise classified (Sign)
      17  RateLimited          too many sign requests from the caller (Sign)
      18  EngineUnavailable    signing engine/security module could not load — deployment (Sign)
      19  InvalidDocument      document to sign is invalid/unreadable — client input (Sign)
      20  EntryExpired         the credential entry window closed before a secret was entered

  Typed results ride on a per-operation sub-interface exported on the
  SAME object path. Strict ordering: the sub-interface Result signal
  fires BEFORE Finished.

  Lost-Finished recovery:
    A fast operation can emit its Result + Finished signals BEFORE a
    client subscribes — the client learns the op object path only from
    the originating method's return value, so there is an unavoidable
    window between that return and its match-rule install. The read-only
    terminal properties Completed / Status / ErrorCode let such a client
    recover the outcome race-free: after the method returns, read
    Completed; if true the op already finished and Status + ErrorCode
    carry the same values the missed Finished signal would have. They are
    valid only while Completed == true (the op object survives a short
    cleanup grace window after Finished precisely so this read succeeds),
    and Status / ErrorCode use the same enumerations as Finished's status
    and errorCode arguments.

  Linux-realization-only properties:
    IsIndeterminate and WatchdogTimeoutSeconds describe this agent's
    Linux/PC-SC realization and have no macOS-CryptoTokenKit analogue. A
    cross-platform parity binding need not mirror them; the wire-stable
    contract for parity purposes is Phase, the Finished signal, and the
    Completed / Status / ErrorCode terminal triple.
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.librescrs.Agent.Operation1">
    <property name="Phase"                  type="u" access="read"/>
    <property name="Progress"               type="d" access="read"/>
    <!-- IsIndeterminate: honest spinner flag — true for card-I/O ops that
         have no meaningful completion percentage. Linux-realization-only
         (see the doc block above); not part of the macOS parity surface.
         No client reader yet; reserved for a progress-UI consumer. -->
    <property name="IsIndeterminate"        type="b" access="read"/>
    <!-- WatchdogTimeoutSeconds: Linux-realization-only (see doc block).
         No client reader yet; reserved for diagnostics. -->
    <property name="WatchdogTimeoutSeconds" type="u" access="read"/>
    <!-- Terminal triple for lost-Finished recovery (see doc block). Only
         meaningful while Completed == true. -->
    <property name="Completed"              type="b" access="read"/>
    <property name="Status"                 type="u" access="read"/>
    <property name="ErrorCode"              type="u" access="read"/>

    <method name="Cancel"/>

    <signal name="Finished">
      <arg name="status"      type="u"/>
      <arg name="errorCode"   type="u"/>
      <arg name="msgKey"      type="s"/>
      <arg name="msgFallback" type="s"/>
    </signal>
  </interface>
</node>
